Sure! Here's a simple JavaScript function that will switch the order of adjectives in a given sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(a|an|the)\s+[a-z]+$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
  const newSentence = "";

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (adjectives.includes(words[i])) {
      newSentence += reversedAdjectives[i] + " ";
    } else {
      newSentence += words[i] + " ";
    }
  }

  return newSentence.trim();
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function first splits the input sentence into individual words using the `split()` method. It then creates an empty array called `adjectives` and loops through each word in the sentence. If the current word is an adjective (starting with "a", "an", or "the" followed by one or more alphabetical characters), it is added to the `adjectives` array.

Once all the adjectives have been identified, the function reverses their order using the `reverse()` method and then loops through the original sentence again. For each word, if it is an adjective, the corresponding reversed adjective is added to the new sentence, otherwise, the original word is added. Finally, the function trims any leading or trailing whitespace from the new sentence and returns it.

In the example above, the input sentence "Beautiful green trees are in the park" is successfully transformed into "Green beautiful trees are in the park".

