This error occurs when the user enters an invalid input, such as a non-numeric value, while the program is expecting a numerical input. To handle this exception, you can use a try-catch block to catch the InputMismatchException and prompt the user to enter a valid input. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this exception:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int num1 = 0;
        int num2 = 0;
        try {
            System.out.print("Enter the first number: ");
            num1 = scanner.nextInt();
            System.out.print("Enter the second number: ");
            num2 = scanner.nextInt();
            int sum = num1 + num2;
            System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is " + sum);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid number.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume the invalid input
            main(args); // Recursively call the method to prompt the user for valid input
        }
    }
}
```
In this modified code, if the user enters an invalid input, the program will catch the InputMismatchException and print an error message. It will then consume the invalid input using `scanner.nextLine()` and recursively call the `main` method to prompt the user for valid input. This will continue until the user enters a valid input.

