Yes, you can divide each hour into 10 equal parts by creating 10 equally spaced data points within each hour. Here's a simple formula in Excel that you can use:

Assuming your data is in column A, starting in cell A2, enter the following formula in cell B2:

`=A2+((1/10)-(MOD(A2,1)))`

This formula adds 1/10 to the current value (A2) and subtracts the remainder of dividing the current value by 1, which ensures that the resulting value falls between 0 and 1. This will create a new data point within the same hour as the original value, but 1/10th of the way through the hour. Copy this formula down for the rest of the hours in column A.

In MATLAB, you can achieve the same result using the `linspace` function:

```MATLAB
% Assuming your data is in a vector called 'data'
num_points = length(data);
hourly_points = zeros(num_points, 10);

for i = 1:num_points
    current_time = data(i);
    hour = floor(current_time / 3600); % Convert to whole hours

    % Create 10 equally spaced points within the hour
    start_time = hour * 3600;
    end_time = start_time + 3600;
    time_steps = linspace(start_time, end_time, 10);

    hourly_points(i, :) = time_steps;
end
```

This code will create a new matrix called `hourly_points` with 10 columns for each hour in the input data vector 'data'. The rows represent the individual data points within each hour.

