To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` class and the `GetClaim` method to extract the claim with the desired username. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System;
using System.Security.Claims;
using System.Text;

public class JwtService
{
    private readonly SymmetricSecurityKey _key;

    public JwtService(string key)
    {
        _key = new SymmetricSecurityKey(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(key));
    }

    public string GenerateToken(string username)
    {
        var claims = new[]
        {
            new Claim(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Sub, username),
            new Claim(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Iss, "your_issuer"),
            new Claim(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Exp, DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddHours(1).ToUnixTimeSeconds()),
            new Claim(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Iat, DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeSeconds())
        };

        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var token = tokenHandler.CreateToken(new SecurityTokenDescriptor
        {
            Issuer = "your_issuer",
            Expires = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddHours(1),
            SigningCredentials = new SigningCredentials(_key, SecurityAlgorithms.Hmac256)
        });

        return tokenHandler.WriteToken(token);
    }

    public string GetUsernameFromToken(string token)
    {
        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var tokenSigned = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
            IssuerSigningKey = _key,
            ValidateIssuer = false,
            ValidateAudience = false
        });

        var usernameClaim = tokenSigned.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Sub);
        return usernameClaim?.Value;
    }

Inner and outer functions are terms used in the context of functional programming. Inner functions are functions that are defined within another function, while outer functions are functions that are defined outside of any other function.

Inner functions can access the variables and parameters of their outer function, but they cannot be called directly from outside the outer function. Outer functions can be called directly from outside the function, and they can access the variables and parameters of their inner functions if they are declared with the `let` or `var` keyword.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference between inner and outer functions:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let outerVariable = "outer";

  function innerFunction() {
    console.log(outerVariable); // outputs "outer"
  }

  return innerFunction;
}

const innerFunction = outerFunction();
innerFunction(); // outputs "outer"
```
In this example, `outerFunction` is an outer function that defines an inner function `innerFunction`. The `innerFunction` has access to the `outerVariable` defined in the `outerFunction`, and it can modify its value. The `outerFunction` returns the `innerFunction`, which can then be called directly from outside the `outerFunction`.

