SQL is a relational database language, and it doesn't provide functionality for creating reminders or performing actions outside of the database. However, you can use SQL to schedule a job that will check your work sessions and prompt you to take a break if necessary. You can use a scheduling tool like cron (for Unix-based systems) or Task Scheduler (for Windows) to run this job at regular intervals.

Here's an example of a cron job that checks your work sessions every minute and prompts you to take a break if 10 minutes have passed since the start time:
```bash
#!/bin/bash

while true; do
    sqlite3 your_database.db "SELECT * FROM work_sessions WHERE start_time IS NOT NULL AND end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;";
    current_session=$(sqlite3 your_database.db "SELECT * FROM work_sessions WHERE start_time IS NOT NULL AND end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ORDER BY start_time DESC LIMIT 1;");

    if [ -n "$current_session" ]; then
        start_time=$(echo $current_session | awk '{print $2}');
        end_time=$(echo $current_session | awk '{print $3}');
        elapsed_time=$(date --date="$end_time" +%s) - $(date --date="$start_time" +%s);

        if (( elapsed_time >= 600 )); then
            echo "It's time to take a break!"
        fi
    fi

    sleep 1;
done
```
This script will continuously check your work sessions and prompt you to take a break if 10 minutes have passed since the start time. You can adjust the interval (in seconds) between checks by changing the `sleep` command.

Note that this solution assumes that you have a SQLite database file named "your_database.db" and that your work sessions are stored in a table named "work_sessions". You may need to modify the script to fit your specific database and table names.

To create a visualizer for an audio file in Unity, you can use the AudioSource component and create a custom script to analyze the audio data. Here's a simple example:

1. Create a new C# script called "AudioVisualizer".
2. Add the following code to the script:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class AudioVisualizer : MonoBehaviour
{
    public AudioSource audioSource;
    public float frequencyThreshold = 0.5f;
    public Color[] colors = new Color[10];

    private float[] audioData;
    private int sampleRate;

    void Start()
    {
        audioSource = GetComponent<AudioSource>();
        sampleRate = audioSource.sampleRate;
        audioData = new float[sampleRate];
    }

    void Update()
    {
        audioSource.GetSpectrumData(audioData, 0, FFTWindow.Blackman);

        float maxAmplitude = 0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < audioData.Length; i++)
        {
            float amplitude = Mathf.Abs(audioData[i]);
            if (amplitude > maxAmplitude)
            {
                maxAmplitude = amplitude;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < audioData.Length; i++)
        {
            float normalizedAmplitude = audioData[i] / maxAmplitude;
            int colorIndex = (int)(normalizedAmplitude * colors.Length);
            transform.GetChild(i).GetComponent<Renderer>().material.color = colors[colorIndex];
        }
    }
}
```
This script uses the AudioSource component to get the audio spectrum data. It then normalizes the amplitude of each frequency and assigns a color to each frequency based on its amplitude. The colors are defined in the `colors` array.

To use this script, attach it to a GameObject in your scene and set the AudioSource component to the audio file you want to visualize. You can also adjust the `frequencyThreshold` variable to control the sensitivity of the visualization.

Note that this is a basic example and you may need to adjust it to fit

