Sure! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates natural selection. We'll use a population of virtual organisms with different traits and select the fittest individuals for reproduction.

```matlab
% Define the population size
population_size = 100;

% Define the number of generations
num_generations = 100;

% Define the traits of the organisms
traits = rand(population_size, 1); % Random values between 0 and 1

% Define the fitness function
fitness = @(traits) sum(traits); % Sum of the traits

% Initialize the population
parents = zeros(population_size, 1);
for i = 1:population_size
    parents(i) = traits(i);
end

% Simulate natural selection
for generation = 1:num_generations
    % Evaluate the fitness of the parents
    fitness_values = fitness(parents);

    % Select the fittest parents for reproduction
    selected_parents = parents(sort(fitness_values, 'descend'));

    % Create the offspring by combining the traits of the selected parents
    offspring = zeros(population_size, 1);
    for i = 1:population_size
        offspring(i) = selected_parents(i) * 0.5 + parents(i) * 0.5;
    end

    % Replace the parents with the offspring
    parents = offspring;
end

% Plot the evolution of the population over time
figure;
plot(1:num_generations, fitness(parents));
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Fitness');
title('Natural Selection Simulation');
```

This code simulates the evolution of a population of virtual organisms over 100 generations. The fitness of each individual is determined by the sum of its traits, and the fittest individuals are selected for reproduction. Over time, the population becomes more fit as the advantageous traits become more common.

