Yes, when the server sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `\*`, it allows requests from any origin, including those without an authorization header. This is done to ensure that the server is accessible to all clients, regardless of their location or credentials.

If you want to include an authorization header in your requests, you can do so by setting the `Authorization` header in your client (e.g., browser or API client). The server will then receive the authorization header and can use it to authenticate your request.

Alternatively, you can configure the server to only allow requests from specific origins and to require an authorization header for those requests. This can be done by setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a specific domain or IP address, and by requiring the `Authorization` header in the server's configuration.

Here's an example of how you can set the `Authorization` header in a browser using JavaScript:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com', {
  headers: {
    Authorization: 'Bearer your_access_token'
  }
});
```
And here's an example of how you can set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header in a server using Node.js and Express:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'https://example.com');
  next();
});

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello, world!');
});

app.listen(3000);
```
In this example, the server is configured to only allow requests from `https://example.com`, and to require an authorization header for those requests. The `Authorization` header is set to `Bearer your_access_token`, which is a sample access token.

